    "It might be hard right now, but every moment you spend panting and sweating will become part of you, Participation Award! If you don't believe me, let me tell you about when I went to the outskirts! Speaking of participation awards, that reminds me of this invention contest a while back, back then, you know, I still remember. I made a feather-powered clockwork automatic toothpick dispenser and entered it. When people asked what it was, I told them proudly. This isn't technology. It's art. Sure, everyone tilted their heads, but one of the judges patted my shoulder and said, \"What a truly useless invention.\" Then he smiled and gave me an award. A participation award. That award is sitting in a corner of my room. Well, calling it a room is a bit of a stretch. It's turned into a multipurpose room that's a walk-in closet and a study and a storage room and also has a cat litter box. Oh right, speaking of cats, my cat's name is Invincible Supreme King Super-Duper Kitty. Invincible for short. Name's too long? Can't help it. My nephew and I spent ages deciding when we first named him. My nephew wanted Thor but I wanted something stronger. Invincible wakes me up every morning at 4 a.m. He sits on my eyelids. Physically. Actually literally hoists his butt right onto my eyelids. So these days I wake up at 4 a.m. every day. It's surprisingly nice, that hour, quiet. Oh, speaking of early mornings, I like being alone in the living room with the lights off and opening the fridge just a crack to look for things by its light. In that moment I feel like some kind of explorer, you know? I saw it in a documentary once. People who train their night vision using fridge light. That's me. I'm that person. Also, what brand do you guys drink? For a while I only drank Ice Mountain Spring Water in 500ml plastic bottles, the cap color was kind of charming. But lately I got a water purifier at home. Only after getting it did I realize. With a purifier, the filter really is everything. If you don't replace the filter it just tastes like that slightly earthy well water I used to scoop up at my grandma's place as a kid. Speaking of the countryside"
